---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const repo = 'https://github.com/omkar-k0/cuddly-octo-palm-tree';
const blob = `${repo}/blob/main`;
const demo = 'https://cuddly-octo-palm-tree-ashen.vercel.app';

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '—', label: 'accuracy on real legal questions: no live run, and no lawyer has checked the labels yet', evidence: 'pending', source: 'README, Eval; QUESTIONS.md #1–#2' },
  { value: '21 of 30', label: 'right in a mock run, where keyword rules and templates stand in for the AI models', evidence: 'synthetic', source: 'npm run eval -- --mode mock at commit 0953e17, rerun 26 Nov 2026' },
  { value: '110', label: 'sections of Indian law in the library, across 4 areas, each cited by its source file', evidence: 'measured', source: 'sources/ at commit 0953e17' },
  { value: '203', label: 'automated tests, all passing', evidence: 'measured', source: 'npx vitest run at commit 0953e17, 26 Nov 2026' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Web app: legal first information for India' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'November 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Product owner: scope, decisions and review. An AI model (Claude) wrote most of the code, as the commit history shows' },
  { label: 'Scale', value: '4 areas of law · 110 sections from India Code · 30 test questions' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Live demo · not tested on real cases · no lawyer review yet' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'broke', label: 'What broke' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Dem Fragen"
  question="What does Indian law say about my problem, and when do I need a lawyer?"
  description="A web app that answers a legal question in plain words, cites the exact section of Indian law it relies on, and says when to see an advocate. Evaluated so far only on a labelled mock run."
  crumb="Product · 09"
  links={[
    { label: 'Open the live demo', href: demo, primary: true },
    { label: 'Source on GitHub', href: repo },
    { label: 'Eval method', href: `${blob}/eval/README.md` },
  ]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  tldr={{
    why: 'Most people in India cannot easily ask a lawyer a first question. A cited first answer, with a clear "see an advocate now" signal, is useful and safe only if every claim points to real law.',
    problem: 'Someone with a problem at work, with a landlord or with a company does not know what the law says, which law applies to their date and state, or when they need a lawyer.',
    result: 'A live app that cites only sections it actually retrieved and escalates criminal, urgent or unsure cases. It has not been tested on real cases: the only evaluation is a mock run on 30 questions, and the same AI model wrote the system and the test.',
    role: 'Product owner. I set the scope and made the decisions; an AI model wrote most of the code.',
  }}
  reproduce={{
    repo,
    commands: `git clone ${repo} && cd cuddly-octo-palm-tree
npm ci
npx vitest run                   # 203 tests
npm run eval -- --mode mock      # the labelled mock baseline (no API key needed)
npm run dev                      # http://localhost:3000, mock mode without ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
    files: [
      { path: 'eval/questions.jsonl', what: 'The 30 labelled questions, written by Claude, not yet reviewed by a lawyer' },
      { path: 'eval/run-eval.ts', what: 'Routing, citation, escalation and per-confidence-band accuracy' },
      { path: 'PLAN.md', what: 'The answer pipeline, the gate and the data rules' },
      { path: 'QUESTIONS.md', what: 'Every decision made so far, and what is still open' },
    ],
    note: 'A live run needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Results are written to eval/results/, which is not committed.',
  }}
  stack={['Next.js', 'TypeScript', 'Claude (Haiku, Opus)', 'BM25 retrieval', 'Postgres / PGlite', 'Vitest', 'Vercel']}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      You describe a problem in your own words: "HR says I get no gratuity after 5 years 8 months". Dem Fragen
      works out which area of law it is, finds the sections that apply to when and where it happened, and explains
      where you stand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every point in the answer links to the exact section it comes from. If the case is criminal, a deadline is
      close, or the app is unsure, it says plainly: see an advocate.
    </p>
    <p>It is legal information, not legal advice, and every answer says so.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">Can a first answer about Indian law be useful without ever citing law it did not actually read?</p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Asking a chatbot</strong> gives a fluent answer that may cite an Act or section that does not exist,
      or one that was repealed. The reader cannot tell which.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Searching the law yourself</strong> fails on wording. People write "laid off with no notice"; the
      statute says "retrenchment".
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One answer for everyone</strong> ignores time and place. India replaced its old labour Acts with new
      Codes on 21 November 2025, and rent law differs by state. The same question can have two right answers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>How it works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Scrub</strong> names, phone numbers and ID numbers before any model sees the question.</li>
      <li><strong>Classify</strong> (a small, fast model): area of law, criminal or not, deadline, date and state, plus legal search terms.</li>
      <li><strong>Retrieve</strong> the best-matching sections, keeping only law in force for that date and place.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer</strong> (a larger model, JSON only), citing retrieved passages by id. The model never names an Act; the server fills the Act and section from the source file.</li>
      <li><strong>Gate</strong> on the server: drop any citation that was not retrieved; with no valid citation, mark the answer unverifiable; escalate below 0.6 confidence, for criminal cases, or with a deadline under 7 days.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="shot">
      <img src="/shots/demfragen-answer.webp" width="1280" height="740" loading="lazy"
        alt="A Dem Fragen answer in mock mode for a gratuity question from Pune, August 2025: law applied for events in August 2025 in Maharashtra, a summary citing Section 4 of the Payment of Gratuity Act and Section 2(s) of the Industrial Disputes Act, three next steps, and a sources panel marking both sections pending verification." />
      <figcaption class="caption">An answer in mock mode, labelled "Mock answer": keyword rules and a template stand in for the models, while retrieval, the date and state rules and the gate are real. Captured from a local build of the repository at commit 0953e17. Both sources show "Pending verification" because no lawyer has reviewed them yet.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Decisions, and what they cost</h2>
    <div class="decisions">
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>The model cites ids, the server names the law</strong>
          <p>A citation can only point to a section the system actually retrieved.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Let the model write citations</strong>
          <p>Shorter code, but an invented section looks exactly like a real one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Choose the law by date and place</strong>
          <p>Each source carries when it was in force and where. Gratuity for March 2025 retrieves the old Act; for January 2026, the new Code.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Only the current law</strong>
          <p>Simpler, but wrong for anyone whose problem started before 21 November 2025.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Ship with sources marked "pending verification"</strong>
          <p>Lawyers verify each source later from a dashboard; a rejected source is never retrieved again.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Wait for a lawyer before going live</strong>
          <p>Safer on paper, but nothing to show a lawyer. Cost: every answer carries an unverified label.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>A labelled mock mode</strong>
          <p>The whole pipeline runs and is tested without an API key, and the UI says "Mock".</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Block on the API key</strong>
          <p>Nothing could be built or tested until it arrived.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>What came out</h2>
    <p>
      A live app covering four areas (corporate, employment, real estate, technology and IP) with 110 sections from
      India Code, a lawyer verification dashboard and 203 automated tests.
    </p>
    <h3>The only evaluation so far: a mock run</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Mock run, 30 questions</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Routed to the right area</td><td class="n">29 of 30</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Answers with at least one valid citation</td><td class="n">26 of 26</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expected source retrieved</td><td class="n">18 of 20</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expected source cited</td><td class="n">14 of 20</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Escalation matches the label</td><td class="n">27 of 30</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All of the above right</td><td class="n">21 of 30</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="caption">Source: <code>npm run eval -- --mode mock</code> at commit 0953e17, rerun 26 Nov 2026; same as the README table of 15 Nov. In mock mode, keyword rules and templates replace both models, so this tests retrieval, the rules and the gate, not answer quality.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="broke">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What broke, and what is unproven</h2>
    <h3>Confidence is not calibrated</h3>
    <p>
      In the mock run, answers in the 0.6–0.8 confidence band were right on 1 of 5, while the high band (0.8–1.0) was right on 16 of 21. A
      well-calibrated system gets more accurate as confidence rises; this one does not yet. The live models may differ.
    </p>
    <h3>The old labour Acts are paraphrases</h3>
    <p>
      India Code no longer hosts the repealed Acts (Payment of Gratuity, Industrial Disputes and others). The files
      for them are written by hand, not official text, until an official source is found.
    </p>
    <h3>Unproven</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Untested on real cases.</strong> No live run yet: it needs an API key.</li>
      <li><strong>A model wrote both the system and its test.</strong> The 30 questions and their labels came from the same AI model as the code, which flatters the scores. A lawyer has not reviewed them.</li>
      <li>The explanation text itself is not graded, only area, escalation and citations.</li>
      <li>Name redaction is heuristic: a bare name with no cue can get through.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
